Data Evaluation: Theory and Practice for Materials Properties — Ronald G. Munro (@mat-data-book: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_7N31EWH1JVE54EM3BRSQGYS04K
Ronald G. Munro. *Data Evaluation: Theory and Practice for Materials Properties*. NIST Special Publication 960-11, June 2003. [Complete public book](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication960-11.pdf).

Read the whole book progressively. Begin with data evaluation and the meaning of a property; use chapters 3–4 when comparing reported numbers, then carry the later examples of procedural and system-dependent properties into selection and research. A test result needs its specimen, conditions, method and interpretation before it can support a new use.

The browser exposed the scanned 136-page PDF, whose bibliographic extent is 128 pages. Contents and selected chapters 3–4 were inspected on 30 October 2026; direct download returned 403. This is an official free NIST publication. Preserve credited components; free government-hosted access does not establish a blanket licence for every incorporated figure. The book's distinctions guide evidence assessment and do not add a classification feature to Lince.

